# Iteration 1

## DEC-001 — Capture-time floor = A; optional taps (B) are surface-config, not schema
**Decision:** The required capture-time field set is `text`, `created_at`, `tz`, `source`, `author`. `horizon`/`tags` are optional + derivable. Offering optional capture-time taps ("B") is a capture-surface configuration, fully reversible without schema change.
**Rationale (Levi):** Liked B to test but wanted to revoke later if needed; making B a surface concern (not a schema concern) guarantees reversibility.
**Rejected:** C (required horizon at capture) — too much friction, violates the friction guarantee.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q1.

## DEC-002 — Source-specific context = `source` + optional source-shaped `context` object (option 2)
**Decision:** `source` is an always-present simple channel tag; an optional `context` object (shape keyed to source) carries channel-specific data — for `claude-hook`: `{kind: "claude-session", session_id, seq, parent_id?}`. Absent for channels with no context.
**Rationale (Levi):** Need to reconstruct a Claude session's prompts in order; field unused by other channels; sparse-optional fits. Chose (2) over flat fields (1) and generic list (3); designed to grow object→list into (3) later.
**Note:** Populated by the prompt-capture-hook (part 3); schema reserves the shape in v1. Claude-prompt drops are user entries (author=user) → in v1 scope.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q2.

## DEC-003 — Scope boundary: this part = horizontal time-stream (data lake) only; entity store is OUT
**Decision:** This part builds one database = the core of the **horizontal, time-anchored implementation stream** (append-only data lake). v1 holds user entries; later, more stream-item kinds (e.g. system events) on the same timeline. The **vertical entity/"things" store** (derived mart/strata complementing the lake) is a **separate, future database — out of scope here.**
**Consequence:** Drop the `entity_axis` facet from the schema (amends DP-1 §2, which listed it). "Everything is an entry" = everything in this store is a stream item.
**Rationale (Levi):** The things/entities layer is outside this part's scope; this part is just the horizontal stream.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q3.

## DEC-004 — Defer the stream-item-kind discriminator (no `kind` field in v1)
**Decision:** Do not add a `kind`/`entity_axis`/`user_entry` discriminator field in v1. Future stream-item kinds (system events) will be distinguishable via `source`/`tags`; the explicit mechanism is decided when those arrive.
**Rationale (Levi):** Multiple valid ways to model it; don't pre-decide. A dedicated field now is premature and possibly redundant given `source` exists.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q3 follow-up.

## DEC-005 — Physical store = JSONL for v1; logical schema kept storage-agnostic
**Decision:** v1 physical store = JSONL (append-dominant; edit semantics in DEC-006). The logical entry (self-contained JSON object, stable `id`, UTC timestamps) is designed storage-agnostic. Migration ladder preserved: JSONL → SQLite (if local query speed bites) → Postgres/Supabase (if hosting/server/entity-store wanted). All three preserve the same logical schema. Capture/consumers go through a thin storage seam (`append_entry`/`read_day`/`query`), not scattered raw file-reads.
**Rationale (Levi + orchestrator):** JSONL is ideal for the local single-user append-only horizontal lake; does NOT lock out Supabase (Postgres JSONB ingests entries directly). Adopting a server now would break the local/private posture prematurely. Maps to Levi's lake→strata framing (lake=JSONL files; future vertical entity store=Postgres-shaped).
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q5 (storage substrate).

## DEC-006 — Edits = in-place corrections; no revision/lineage; "append-only" relaxed
**Decision:** Stream-entry edits are **in-place corrections** (rewrite the entry, bump `modified`). **No `lineage_id`, no `supersedes`, no revision history.** Versioned history is an *entity* concern → the vertical store → out of scope (DEC-003). Physical model relaxed from strict append-only to **append-on-drop, edit-in-place-on-correction**.
**Rationale (Levi):** Edits here are only corrections to user entries (like the Excel Dropper), not versions of things; doesn't want lineage complexity. "Append-only" was orchestrator over-spec; its only payoff (immutable history) isn't wanted here.
**Tradeoff (flagged):** in-place edits reintroduce a small multi-surface/iCloud sync-conflict surface vs strict append-only; acceptable for single-user/low-frequency; mitigate by day-partitioning the file later if it bites.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q4.

## DEC-007 — Excel stays a valid capture channel; minimal computer dropper+view pulled forward (op-path change)
**Decision:** The Excel Dropper remains a **valid capture channel during transition** (`source: dropper-excel`; text+timestamp is a complete entry, rich fields derive) via an Excel→JSONL ingest. A **minimal "computer dropper + editable table view over the JSONL"** is pulled forward in the operative path (right after schema + mobile), split out from the full filing-UX app-wrapper (which stays last). Excel is retired only once the minimal surface exists.
**Rationale (Levi):** Today's Excel does 3 jobs — computer capture, whole-stream view, edit-older-entries; the op-path only restored these at the *last* part, stranding him mid-transition. Real gap he surfaced.
**Scope note:** The surface itself is an orchestration-level part (new/re-sequenced), NOT the schema spec. The schema spec's migration plan must bake in "Excel stays a capture channel; don't strand computer-drop." Recorded to DP-1 PROGRESS as a gap + op-path revision.
**Revised migration order:** schema → (Excel keeps writing, ingested) → mobile shortcut → minimal computer dropper+table-view → retire Excel → … → full wrapper last.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q6.

## DEC-008 — `tags` kept, but demoted to optional non-authoritative ad-hoc labels
**Decision:** Keep `tags` in v1 as an **optional, free-form, non-authoritative** array — ad-hoc labels for crude filtering/associating the *raw stream* before the entity layer exists. NOT a taxonomy, NOT load-bearing for typed retrieval (the future entity store owns authoritative typing), absent-when-unused, no committed vocabulary. When entities own real typing, tags fade or linger informally — no migration (never authoritative).
**Rationale (Levi):** Authoritative typing belongs on the entities the stream spawns, not the raw entry; but ad-hoc labels have standalone present utility (only alternative is full-text search). Demote, don't drop.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q7.

## DEC-009 — Guiding principle: the horizontal stream stays thin (+ `tier` deferred)
**Principle (adopted, Levi):** *The horizontal stream stays deliberately thin. Typing, structure, and entities accrete in the downstream vertical layer — not on the raw entry.* This is the rule for all remaining field decisions; it's why `entity_axis` (DEC-003), `kind` (DEC-004), and authoritative typing (DEC-008) live downstream.
**Immediate consequence:** **`tier`/persistence-retention deferred/out of v1** — it's a downstream lifecycle/cleanup concern (de-weighting, retention), not a property of the raw entry. The stream keeps everything; tiering happens downstream later. (Supersedes §2's persistence-tier scope-shape.)
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q7 follow-up.

## DEC-010 — `id` = canonical, store-minted UUIDv7; surface-local ids deferred; dedupe off the schema
**Decision:** Each entry's canonical `id` is **minted on append by the store seam** (`append_entry`), not by the capture surface. Format = **UUIDv7** (time-ordered: free chronological sort + index locality on the future Postgres rung; standardized RFC 9562; drops natively into a Postgres `uuid` column — ULID would not). Dedupe is **not a schema concern**; the only real need is Excel-bridge idempotency (see DEC-011).
**Rationale (Levi):** Questioned my Q8 "surface mints the id" default — that reflex only pays off for offline-first clients that need identity before reaching the store, which v1 doesn't have. Store-seam minting is one place, simpler. Surfaces *may* keep transient local ids and let the core assign the canonical id on append (recognized client-local-id / server-authoritative-id pattern) — preserves a clean offline-first upgrade path without committing to it now. 128-bit size is irrelevant at this scale.
**Deferred:** true offline-first surface-local id reconciliation (more implementation choices there; revisit when a surface needs pre-sync identity).
**Rejected:** surface-minted canonical id (premature); UUIDv4 (no ordering); ULID (not a UUID → poor Postgres-rung fit); content-hash id (breaks on edit — see DEC-011).
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q1-redux.

## DEC-011 — Excel stays Excel-blind to the core: bridge + sidecar, snapshot reconciliation; `modified` ignored & dropped
**Decision:** The core JSONL schema is designed **as if Excel does not exist.** All Excel-specific behavior lives in the **bridge script + a sidecar state file** (`excel-ingest-state.json`) mapping each Excel row (by drop-timestamp, col E) → the JSONL `id` the bridge created + a snapshot of the text last ingested. Idempotency + edit-propagation use **snapshot reconciliation**: per run, new row → append; text changed vs snapshot → rewrite the mapped JSONL entry; unchanged → skip. The bridge reads **only `{text, drop-timestamp (col E)}` and explicitly ignores the `modified` column** (this ignore-rule is recorded protocol → spec section + acceptance criterion). `modified`/`updated_at` is **dropped from v1** (Levi need not edit Excel; the column is simply never read).
**Rationale (Levi):** Excel is being phased out, so it must not dictate core design; keep its quirks (timestamp-keying, sub-second tiebreaker, buggy `modified`) confined to a throwaway adapter. Snapshot reconciliation is correct **only because** the dataset is small (full O(n) re-diff per read is cheap) AND Excel is temporary (we deliberately skip deletion-detection and conflict handling) — Levi affirmed this reasoning; if either condition changed, the approach would change.
**Deferred / known gap:** Excel-row *deletion* is not detected (lingering JSONL entry); acceptable single-user, revisit only if it bites. Incremental/watermark diffing only if Excel ever grows large (it won't, being temporary).
**Rejected:** content-hash dedupe (an Excel text-edit changes the hash → duplicate instead of update); putting Excel↔JSON correlation in an entry field (that's bridge bookkeeping → sidecar); "edit-immediately-or-lose-it" and a manual reingest flag (snapshot reconciliation needs nothing manual).
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q1-redux. Refines DEC-007 (Excel-as-channel) and DEC-006 (in-place edits).

## DEC-012 — Auxiliary-field family: `meta_notes` (prose) + `source_data` (structured stash), kept distinct from `tags`/`context`
**Decision:** Add two optional flex fields, the entry's "schema-doesn't-have-to-commit-early" tier:
- **`meta_notes`** (string, prose) — unstructured human/AI annotations *about* the entry. Safe because it's prose (can't silently become a queryable schema). **Convention:** append-only, one line each, `ISO-timestamp — free prose`, newest at bottom; **no typed prefixes/categories.** **Tripwire:** wanting typed prefixes = signal to promote to `source_data` or a real field.
- **`source_data`** (object) — the **structured stash / schema-on-read**: preserve structured remainder *from the source* verbatim when it doesn't warrant core fields — neither discard (lossy) nor promote-everything (rigid); promote a field later with history intact. Optional, reserved in v1, not required to populate; first populator = claude-hook session payload (part 3).
These join the existing aux fields, each with a distinct job: **`tags`** = ad-hoc labels (DEC-008) · **`meta_notes`** = prose about the entry · **`context`** = *designed* source-shaped context (DEC-002) · **`source_data`** = *undesigned* raw source remainder. (`context` = structure we designed; `source_data` = structure we refuse to lose.) Excel↔JSON correlation lives in the sidecar, not any field.
**Rationale (Levi):** A thin stream + AI means the schema needn't commit early; prose notes and a lossless raw-stash are the pressure-release valves (pure lake instinct — DEC-005/009). Levi's articulation of `source_data` (lossless schema-on-read) confirmed exactly right. Naming `source_data` chosen over `raw` (ambiguous) / `extra`.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q1-redux. Aligns with DEC-008 (tags), DEC-002 (context), DEC-009 (thin-stream).

## DEC-013 — checkin-compat: JSONL becomes source of truth on first ingest; read-seam contract; one-time tz backfill
**Decision:** `/checkin`'s read path moves to JSONL **as soon as the DB is viable** (= Excel ingested → JSONL is a superset of Excel), not kept on Excel. This spec commits only the **read-seam contract**: `read_day(date)` exists, buckets entries by **local-day-of-offset `created_at`** (root-fixes the tz mis-bucketing), and preserves **output parity** with `read_dropper_day.py` ({text, time} per day). The actual repointing of `read_dropper_day.py` (swap guts xlsm→JSONL, keep CLI/output identical) and any **read-side `source` filter** (to keep hook drops from flooding the daily view) are **op-path / consumer concerns**, not schema shape.
**Legacy timezone = one-time first-ingest backfill (not ongoing).** Excel datetimes are naive (serial numbers, no tz). Levi is EDT for the foreseeable future ⇒ all ongoing Excel drops are EDT (bridge stamps EDT, no logic); the MDT minority lives only in the legacy 852 rows ⇒ deconflicted **once**, with care (AI-assisted at that single moment). The Excel file is itself the legacy backup while it lives, so **no `source_data` timestamp stash needed** for tz. `read_day` stays dumb (buckets by whatever offset is on `created_at`).
**Rationale (Levi):** Flipping checkin to JSONL immediately eliminates any lossy window (channels writing JSONL while checkin reads Excel) — relevant because a non-Excel channel (likely the hook) goes live ~today. tz deconfliction is inherently one-time because timezone won't change again before Excel is deprecated. Keep everything else unbloated.
**Deferred:** `occurred_at` back-dating + cross-day record association → strata layer (flexible in skill usage until then; not bolted onto read_day).
**Dropped:** Excel-row deletion detection (single-user, narrow, not worth the code).
**Hook-go-live notes (not schema changes):** sanity-check the hook can emit the reserved `context` shape (DEC-002); add a read-side `source` filter so `/checkin` doesn't surface every Claude prompt.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q2-redux. Realizes the orchestration hard-constraint "must keep feeding read_dropper_day.py / checkin."

## DEC-014 — `created_at` = ISO-8601 + offset; `occurred_at`/`planned_for` cut; `horizon` folded into `tags`
**Decision:** `created_at` (the one required time) is stored as **ISO-8601 with explicit offset** (e.g. `2026-06-24T15:42:00-04:00`) — instant + local offset in a single string; root-fixes tz mis-bucketing, sorts lexicographically, maps to Postgres `timestamptz`. **IANA zone name is deferred** (the offset pins the instant and the local day; a named zone is only needed for future DST-correct forward-dated reasoning, and is additive when wanted). **`occurred_at` and `planned_for` are cut from v1.** **`horizon` is dropped as a dedicated field and folded into `tags`** (a plan/projected drop is just a tag).
**Rationale (Levi):** Levi pause-checked that he never specified `occurred_at`/`planned_for`/`horizon` — correct: they were inherited from the DP-1 orchestration baseline, not chosen in this interview. Cutting them is consistent with established decisions: they'd be fields with **no v1 consumer** (checkin buckets by `created_at`), and DEC-013 already deferred `occurred_at` back-dating to the strata layer; thin-stream (DEC-009) says when-it-happened-vs-when-logged is a downstream record concern. With the backing times gone, `horizon`'s derivation basis collapses → it's a bare label → belongs in `tags` (DEC-008). Nothing is lost: back-dating can ride `meta_notes`/`source_data` informally until the strata exists, and the cut fields are purely additive later.
**Supersedes/refines:** DEC-001 — its capture-floor decision stands; the part keeping `horizon` as an optional schema field is withdrawn.
**Future promotions (noted, not built):** `occurred_at`, `planned_for`, `horizon` are the natural strata-era field promotions.
**Context:** via /spec interview (iteration 1), Q3+Q4-redux. ISO+offset explicitly approved by Levi; field cuts from Levi's own pause-check; `horizon`→`tags` fold treated-confirmed with veto offered.

## DEC-015 — Transition-window ingest cadence = on-demand at read-time (no daemon/cron), scoped to the bridge
**Decision:** While Excel remains a live channel, the Excel→JSONL ingest runs **on-demand at read-time** (piggybacks `read_day` / consumers); no daemon, no cron. The committed requirement is: no background scheduler + idempotent within a session. The read-time trigger is **scoped to the transition window** — it lives and dies with the bridge; deleting the bridge removes the cadence with it.
**Rationale (Levi):** Surfaced in the interview (Q8) but never recorded as a decision; the seed-comparison experiment recovered it and persona review (contrarian + simplifier) flagged it as both unbacked and mechanism-shaped. Confirmed at /spec revise: on-demand-at-read-time is intended, with the explicit scope that it is transitional, not a permanent load-bearing path — the "this makes the bridge permanent" worry is answered by the bridge's planned retirement (DEC-007).
**Rejected:** explicit/manual ingest step (cleaner read path, but adds a manual action; unnecessary for single-user transitional use); background daemon/cron (over-built for a temporary bridge).
**Context:** via /spec revise (iteration 1), AC4.6; backs the cadence AC folded in during /spec seed. Refines DEC-011/DEC-013 (Excel bridge) and DEC-007 (Excel retirement).

## DEC-016 — Exactly one Dropper Excel file; backs the timestamp row-identity scheme
**Decision:** There is, and will remain, exactly **one** Dropper Excel file feeding the bridge. This is the operating assumption under AC4.2's row-identity scheme: a single file means **stable row order between ingest runs**, so the (drop-timestamp + row-index tiebreaker) key maps the same Excel row to the same JSONL `id` across runs.
**Consequence:** Sub-second timestamp collisions are **not a live concern** (single user, low drop frequency, one file); the row-index tiebreaker is retained as belt-and-suspenders, not load-bearing. No multi-file merge or row-reorder reconciliation is in scope.
**Rationale (Levi):** Confirmed at /spec revise — "won't happen with keeping only one dropper excel file." The timestamp identity holds precisely because nothing re-sorts the rows.
**Context:** via /spec revise (iteration 1), AC4.2. Backs DEC-011 (Excel-blind bridge + sidecar identity) and DEC-016's row-identity assumption in the spec.

## DEC-017 — `created_at` offset correctness is owned at capture; Excel is the bridge-handled channel
**Decision:** Correctness of the `created_at` local offset is the responsibility of each **capture surface**, stamped at write time. The store + read seam (this part) **assumes** a correct offset and neither re-derives nor validates it — AC3.2's local-day bucketing is only as correct as the offset it's handed. **Excel exception (same principle, channel that can't self-stamp):** Excel rows are naive datetimes with no write-time offset, so the **bridge** owns the Excel channel's offset — the legacy 852 rows via the one-time backfill (AC4.4), ongoing pre-deprecation drops stamped EDT — and holds that duty only until Excel is retired.
**Rationale (Levi):** Surfaced by review (ontologist, R2) as a hidden load-bearing assumption: the bucketing guarantee rests on a property owned by out-of-scope capture surfaces with no detection path here. Named as an explicit contract boundary rather than left implicit. Levi's framing: future native surfaces self-stamp; the Excel channel needs the bridge as its offset proxy until retirement — not a carve-out from the rule, but the rule applied to a channel that can't carry its own offset.
**Rejected:** runtime offset validation/detection in this part (over-built for v1 — name the dependency, don't police it); re-deriving offset from a single assumed zone (defeats the explicit-offset design, DEC-014).
**Context:** via /spec revise (iteration 1), R2 review (ontologist most-load-bearing finding). Names a boundary under AC3.2 / success (d); refines DEC-014 (explicit-offset) and DEC-013 (Excel backfill).
